{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pumping lemma for regular languages pumping lemma for regular languages","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md","fileName":"Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Regular Languages and Finite Automata]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pumping lemma for regular languages pumping lemma for regular languages","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md","fileName":"Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages","heading":"Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The pumping lemma states that every regular language has a threshold n such that each accepted string w of length at least n can be written w = xyz with |xy| ≤ n, |y| > 0, and xyᵏz in the language for every k ≥ 0.\n\nThe repeated segment y arises because a finite automaton processing a sufficiently long string must revisit a state. The lemma is used contrapositively: if every proposed threshold admits a long string that cannot be pumped, the language is not regular.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties"